 "rulebook": "World Skate, Skateboarding Technical Commission, Street & Park Competition Rules (update of April 2025): Technical Specifications of Materials, Skateboard (a deck with four wheels attached by trucks; it cannot be physically attached to the skater's feet); the Runs + Tricks format (single tricks, one attempt each); Makes versus Bails (the Head Judge decides whether a trick is landed)",
 "deviations": [
  "Runs + Tricks format: one trick, a stationary ollie on flat ground (the board at rest at the start, no obstacle), the whole board rising at least 0.05 m above the ground, instead of an ollie at speed onto or over a street course's obstacle: 0.05 m is this benchmark's round number (the robot's measured pops lift the board higher), and the stationary form is the one this robot can attempt without a push-off on one leg, which it has no balance for",
  "Makes versus Bails: the Head Judge's call is replaced by fixed criteria (a make: the board leaves the ground with a foot on it and rises clear by 0.05 m, all four wheels come down with both feet on the deck, and the robot stands still on the board for 1.0 s; World Skate judges an ollie made when the rider lands on the board); the judges' score (difficulty, execution, style) is not given",
  "the skateboard is rigid bodies: the deck is three boxes (no concave, no flex), the wheels cylinders, the truck a hinge about its pivot axis with a linear torsion spring for the bushings and a hard stop at 12.0 degrees of deck tilt (the bushings' progressive stiffening is not modelled)",
  "the robot is the size of a child (1.32 m), so the deck is a 7.5 in board, the width skate shops give for that height, instead of an adult's 8 to 8.5 in"
 ],
 "texts": {
  "sentence": "Ollie the skateboard from a standstill: pop the tail, take the board up with your feet until all of it is at least 0.05 m off the ground, land on it and stand still on it for 1.0 s.",
  "rules": "The skateboard: a 0.8 m deck, 0.19 m wide, with a kicktail and a nose turned up 18.0 degrees, on two trucks 0.356 m apart with four 0.026 m-radius wheels; the trucks steer when the deck tilts (pivot axis 63.0 degrees, 100.0 N m/rad of bushing stiffness, a stop at 12.0 degrees of tilt). Nothing holds your feet to it: grip tape (friction 1.0) only. The attempt, in this order: (1) pop: the board leaves the ground (no part of it touching anything but you) with a foot on its deck as it leaves; (2) clear: in the same flight of the board, every part of it (deck, trucks, wheels) is at least 0.05 m above the ground; (3) landed: then all four wheels on the ground (each within 0.005 m of it) with both feet on the deck; (4) stand: for 1.0 s without a break, both feet on the deck (contact breaks of at most 0.05 s), all four wheels on the ground, your torso within about 37 degrees of upright (the cosine of its tilt at least 0.8) and your pelvis slower than 0.35 m/s. You succeed at (4). A pop that does not clear may be followed by another. You fail if any part of you other than the feet and shins touches the board (an ollie is done without hands), if any part of you, a foot included, touches the ground or your pelvis drops below 0.3 m (a bail), if the board touches the ground upside down, after the landing if a foot stays off the deck for more than 0.2 s or your torso tilts beyond that limit, and at 8 s.",
  "venue": "A level concrete plaza. You stand across the board, which is at rest on the ground, facing -y, left foot towards the nose: the board's long axis points along +x, its nose at +x. Your feet are in your standing stance, 0.24 m apart, the left one about the middle of the deck, the right one at the tail's bend; the board's centre is 0.11 m ahead (in x) of your pelvis.",
  "difficulty": "The ollie is a timed whole-body skill on a free board: the tail has to be snapped down with the front foot unloaded, the front foot has to drag the rising board up and level it while the robot is in the air, and the robot has to land on a narrow deck that rolls and steers when it tilts. Even standing still, the board is unstable under a rigid rider (its trucks tilt and steer), so the ankles have to balance it before and after the trick."
 },
